---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ResizeVideo from '@/components/tools/media/ResizeVideo.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="resize-video">
  <ResizeVideo client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to resize a video</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop the video in. The tool reads its current resolution from the file.</li>
      <li>Pick a preset such as 1080p, 720p or 480p. Presets keep the original aspect ratio, so the tool calculates the width.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Custom size…</strong> when you need an exact frame, then decide whether to letterbox, crop or stretch to reach it.</li>
      <li>Change the quality under advanced options if the default CRF 23 does not suit you.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Resize video</strong> and download the result.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Standard video resolutions</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Name</th>
            <th>16:9 size</th>
            <th>Pixels per frame</th>
            <th>Relative encoding cost</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>4K UHD (2160p)</td>
            <td>3840 × 2160</td>
            <td>8.3 M</td>
            <td>4× of 1080p</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>QHD (1440p)</td>
            <td>2560 × 1440</td>
            <td>3.7 M</td>
            <td>1.8×</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Full HD (1080p)</td>
            <td>1920 × 1080</td>
            <td>2.1 M</td>
            <td>1× (reference)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>HD (720p)</td>
            <td>1280 × 720</td>
            <td>0.9 M</td>
            <td>0.44×</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>SD (480p)</td>
            <td>854 × 480</td>
            <td>0.4 M</td>
            <td>0.20×</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mobile (360p)</td>
            <td>640 × 360</td>
            <td>0.23 M</td>
            <td>0.11×</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Letterbox, crop or stretch?</h2>
    <p>
      These options apply only when the target frame has a different shape from the source.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Fit inside and pad</strong> (letterbox) keeps the whole picture and fills the gap with bars. The filter chain is <code>scale=W:H:force_original_aspect_ratio=decrease</code> followed by <code>pad</code>. It is the safest default and the right choice for footage you did not shoot.</li>
      <li><strong>Fill and crop</strong> scales the picture until it covers the frame, then trims the overflow equally from both sides. Use it to turn 16:9 into a 9:16 vertical clip when the subject is centred. When the subject is off centre, it gets cut.</li>
      <li><strong>Stretch</strong> forces the picture into the frame and distorts it. Use it only to correct footage that was squashed to begin with, such as anamorphic material with a broken aspect flag.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Common video sizes</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Where it is going</th>
            <th>Aspect</th>
            <th>Size to enter</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Vertical short-form video</td>
            <td>9:16</td>
            <td>1080 × 1920</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Square social post</td>
            <td>1:1</td>
            <td>1080 × 1080</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Landscape social / web hero</td>
            <td>16:9</td>
            <td>1280 × 720</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Email or docs embed</td>
            <td>16:9</td>
            <td>854 × 480</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why widths and heights must be even</h2>
    <p>
      H.264 and VP9 store colour information at half the resolution of brightness (the 4:2:0 chroma
      layout), so each chroma sample covers a 2 × 2 block of pixels. An odd dimension leaves a half-block
      the encoder cannot represent, and the encoder refuses the job. The tool rounds each value you type
      down to the nearest even number.
    </p>

    <h2>Resizing always re-encodes</h2>
    <p>
      A new pixel grid means each frame is decoded, rescaled with a Lanczos filter and encoded again.
      There is no lossless shortcut, so pick CRF 18-21 if the result must look identical to the source.
      If you only need a smaller file and not a specific resolution, <a href="/compress-video">compress
      video</a> has finer controls.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
